#X text 240 83 Hardware PWM uses a dedicated module on the SoC for
accurate signal generation. Software PWM is handled by the CPU \, so
its timing can vary depending on system load.;
#X obj 230 88 cnv 1 2 45 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 148 Versions <5.0;
#X obj 230 153 cnv 1 2 15 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -221520 -1 0;
#X text 240 173 Supports 2 hardware PWM channels. By default \, the
channels are mapped as follows:;
#X text 240 213 • PWM0 = GPIO12/GPIO18;
#X text 240 238 • PWM1 = GPIO13/GPIO19;
#X text 240 263 Consult the official documentation on how to enable
and configure these.;
#X text 240 303 Note: the Raspberry Pi's analog audio output uses both
PWM channels. Playing audio and simultaneously accessing a PWM channel
may cause issues.;
#X text 240 368 Raspberry Pi 5 and later;
#X obj 230 373 cnv 1 2 15 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -221520 -1 0;
#X text 240 393 Supports 4 hardware PWM channels. By default \, the
channels are mapped as follows:;
#X text 240 433 • PWM0 = GPIO12;
#X text 240 458 • PWM1 = GPIO13;
#X text 240 483 • PWM2 = GPIO18;
#X text 240 508 • PWM3 = GPIO19;
#X text 240 533 Consult the official documentation on how to enable
and configure these.;
#X text 240 583 Some of the GPIO pins capable of supporting hardware
PWM can also be configured for use with other peripherals. Be careful
not to enable two peripherals on the same pin at the same time.;
